1
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:04,640
Hi, I’m Fuzhe.

2
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:10,500
This is ProjectOS,
my final project in Creative Computing.

3
00:00:11,540 --> 00:00:15,160
When I return to an old creative project,

4
00:00:15,720 --> 00:00:19,830
I often need to look through many notes.

5
00:00:21,310 --> 00:00:24,850
I want to remember
what I was trying to do,

6
00:00:25,540 --> 00:00:30,350
what changed,
and what still needs attention.

7
00:00:31,810 --> 00:00:36,030
I developed ProjectOS
to support this process.

8
00:00:39,190 --> 00:00:43,230
Here, I’m using
the Echo Field tutorial example.

9
00:00:43,690 --> 00:00:47,830
The summaries were prepared in advance.

10
00:00:49,230 --> 00:00:54,390
No participant data is shown
in this demo.

11
00:00:55,650 --> 00:00:58,270
The brief has six sections.

12
00:00:59,750 --> 00:01:03,670
They cover the project’s aim, changes,

13
00:01:04,210 --> 00:01:06,550
current and earlier directions,

14
00:01:07,190 --> 00:01:12,250
feedback, open issues,
and possible next steps.

15
00:01:14,910 --> 00:01:16,970
Let’s look at the current aim.

16
00:01:17,850 --> 00:01:21,430
This is the summary that ProjectOS

17
00:01:21,450 --> 00:01:25,270
produced from the project records.

18
00:01:26,930 --> 00:01:31,110
It is a starting point,
not a final answer.

19
00:01:32,220 --> 00:01:38,680
I need to decide whether it
still describes my project.

20
00:01:41,390 --> 00:01:45,390
I can open the supporting evidence here.

21
00:01:45,880 --> 00:01:50,360
These excerpts let me see
what the summary is based on.

22
00:01:51,710 --> 00:01:54,670
I can also open the source details.

23
00:01:55,990 --> 00:02:02,210
But an old record may no longer
describe the current situation.

24
00:02:03,150 --> 00:02:06,090
So I still need to check its meaning.

25
00:02:09,517 --> 00:02:11,217
I have four choices:

26
00:02:11,977 --> 00:02:16,537
Confirm, Update, Reject,
or Not applicable.

27
00:02:16,797 --> 00:02:20,957
For this demo, I’ll choose Update.

28
00:02:22,127 --> 00:02:26,617
Imagine that my focus has changed.

29
00:02:27,857 --> 00:02:30,897
I now want to test whether visitors

30
00:02:30,917 --> 00:02:34,107
understand the movement feedback.

31
00:02:34,127 --> 00:02:37,427
This is a fictional example,

32
00:02:38,177 --> 00:02:41,597
not a change recorded
in the original files.

33
00:02:44,547 --> 00:02:48,107
I enter my update in the
‘Your updated version’ field.

34
00:02:49,157 --> 00:02:52,617
Then I click Save and continue.

35
00:02:53,257 --> 00:02:58,137
The progress now shows
one of six sections reviewed.

36
00:03:00,917 --> 00:03:05,417
After refreshing,
I can return to the first section.

37
00:03:06,017 --> 00:03:08,517
My saved response is still here.

38
00:03:09,097 --> 00:03:11,057
The update is stored separately.

39
00:03:11,867 --> 00:03:16,467
It does not change the original files

40
00:03:16,487 --> 00:03:22,287
or automatically rewrite the summary.

41
00:03:25,007 --> 00:03:28,477
For me, the main idea is simple.

42
00:03:29,307 --> 00:03:31,987
AI offers a starting point.

43
00:03:32,647 --> 00:03:36,247
I can check the evidence, disagree,

44
00:03:37,287 --> 00:03:39,927
and record my own understanding.

45
00:03:41,097 --> 00:03:43,697
ProjectOS supports the review.

46
00:03:44,357 --> 00:03:50,917
The final judgement stays with the person
who knows the project.
